# Version control follows lifetime, not file type.
#
# Tracked: anything that is a *conclusion* -- run summaries, trade-level detail,
# parameter grids, generated reports. Small, and the thing you would actually
# want to look back at.
#
# Ignored: anything reproducible from the tracked inputs plus a command, and
# anything large enough that committing each new vintage would bloat history.

# Exchange downloads, kept byte-for-byte as received. Large, and re-fetchable
# from the source archives.
data/raw/

# data/bars/ and data/features/ WERE ignored, for two good reasons that were
# both overruled deliberately: ~108 MB of append-growing CSV whose every
# refresh commits a fresh copy, and the fact that they are reproducible from
# nothing else once a day ages out.
#
# They are tracked now so the published results can actually be re-run by
# someone who is not us. That is the whole point: a backtest nobody can
# reproduce is a claim, not a result. tvdatafeed's anonymous endpoint serves a
# rolling ~6 weeks and TAIFEX publishes 30 trading days, so these files are the
# only surviving copy of most of the sample.
#
# Note what that means for provenance: data/bars/taifex is derived from a
# PURCHASED TAIFEX time-and-sales archive, data/bars/nyse from IBKR, and the
# tv-sourced series from TradingView -- each under its own terms. The feature
# files are not a shield: spread_1m.csv carries qff_close, tsm_close and
# usdttwd_close, i.e. the vendor prices themselves.

# Equity curves are a cumulative function of the run's own trades.csv, and are
# by far the biggest thing a backtest writes. summary.json and trades.csv from
# the same run ARE tracked.
data/runs/**/equity.csv
data/runs/scratch/

tmp/

__pycache__/
.pytest_cache/
*.pyc

node_modules/
